{"summary":"Rejected the amendment because its stated reserve-boundary root cause does not match the current fixture, and the proposed changes would be redundant or risk masking a different brittle test condition.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-7-9009f857","decision":"reject","rationale":"The amendment's main premise is factually weak: the reproduction fixture already seeds animal reserve mass with a substantial buffer, so hatching is not obviously sitting at a reserve-feasibility boundary. Because of that, adding more initial reserve would be redundant at best and could hide the real cause if there is a genuine intermittent failure. If this test is brittle, the more likely issue is the end-state assertion on juveniles after a 60-day run rather than insufficient starting reserve. A speculative diagnostic message alone is not enough to justify accepting the amendment as written."}]}